Chosen exposure: 30000 us (30 ms).
- Dark-subtracted mean in the 200x200 center ROI: 1808.9 DN, which is 44.2% of full scale.
- Saturated pixels: 0.
- It is the only tested exposure in the 40-70% range. The others reached 12.3% (8.33 ms), 14.7% (10 ms) and 29.5% (20 ms) of full scale.
- Hardware capture_series at 30 ms (16 frames, 200x200 ROI): relative std 0.247%, min-to-max spread about 0.93%.

Flicker strength (std/mean and peak-to-peak/mean of the per-frame ROI means, 16 frames each):
- Short exposure, 1000 us: F_std 22.47%, F_pp 59.11%. The dark-subtracted mean is only 1.4% of full scale.
- Chosen exposure, 30000 us: F_std 0.275%, F_pp 0.953%. The hardware series gave 0.247% for F_std.
- F_std at 1 ms is about 82 times F_std at 30 ms.

Other exposures (F_std / F_pp):

| Exposure | F_std | F_pp |
|---|---|---|
| 8333 us | 4.10% | 11.33% |
| 10000 us | 0.315% | 1.17% |
| 20000 us | 0.281% | 1.16% |

Interpretation:
- The flicker is probably 100 Hz, from 50 Hz mains. 10, 20 and 30 ms are whole multiples of the 10 ms period and are stable. 8.33 ms (the 120 Hz period) still shows 4.1% flicker.
- The shot-noise floor of the ROI mean at 30 ms is 0.012%. The residual 0.27% is well above that, so it comes from lamp or intensity noise or drift, not photon noise.

Caveats:
- Only 16 frames per exposure, so F_pp is a rough figure.
- 15, 16.67 and 25 ms were not captured, and frame timing was not recorded.
- The script loaded only 2 frames from the cap009 series file, so its cap009 numbers are not meaningful.
- Some ROI means from the script differ from the camera-reported center means. At 1000 us the dark-subtracted ROI mean is 58.7 DN, but the camera reported 108.94 DN raw (about 68.9 DN after subtracting the dark). That inconsistency was not resolved, so the 22% short-exposure figure carries some uncertainty. It is still far above the 30 ms value.
